Map the polarity in the port: OUT is active low, so a low level is `GpioSignal::ACTIVE`. The ESP32 example uses
[`esp32_xmpl100_port.hpp`](../examples/esp32/main/esp32_xmpl100_port.hpp) (the GPIO driver with the internal
pull-up).

## Control pins

Each control pin has a defined idle level, and the port drives it there before it enables the pin's output
(handbook KB-HW-018). Configuring a pin as an output first and setting its level afterwards can glitch the part.

| `CtrlPin` | Package pin | Polarity | Idle (`INACTIVE`) level | Before the first read |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Enable` | SHDN_N | `ACTIVE` = powered (SHDN_N high) | low: shut down | Set low, then enable the output; `EnsureInitialized()` powers it |

`GpioSignal::ACTIVE` always means "function asserted"; the port maps it to the electrical level.

## Threads

The driver is not thread-safe. Give each driver object one owner task, or guard every call with one mutex.
